@*
    Sample 02 - a technical guide: the documentation case. Ordered steps, code blocks, inline
    code, a reference table and keyboard keys - the elements rendered markdown produces on a
    docs page. Classless on purpose; the wrapper does all the styling.
*@
<h1>Styling rendered markdown</h1>

<p>This guide wires the typeset stylesheet into an app and tunes it for a docs page. The
result styles any HTML your markdown pipeline emits: headings, lists, tables and code, all
from the design tokens your components already use.</p>

<h2>Setup</h2>

<ol>
    <li>Copy <code>typeset.css</code> next to your main stylesheet and import it after
        Tailwind.</li>
    <li>Wrap rendered content in a <code>typeset</code> container.</li>
    <li>Layer a preset class over the wrapper to retune it per context.</li>
</ol>

<pre><code>@("@import \"tailwindcss\";")
@("@import \"./typeset.css\";")

.typeset-docs {
  --typeset-size: 15px;
  --typeset-flow: 1.5em;
}</code></pre>

<p>The wrapper carries the knobs; the content stays untouched:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;div class="typeset typeset-docs"&gt;
  &lt;!-- rendered markdown --&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>

<h2>The knobs</h2>

<p>Three custom properties drive every derived value. Set them on the wrapper, a preset
class, or inline for a one-off:</p>

<table>
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>Property</th>
            <th>Default</th>
            <th>Controls</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td><code>--typeset-size</code></td>
            <td><code>1em</code></td>
            <td>Base text size; headings and captions derive from it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>--typeset-leading</code></td>
            <td><code>1.75</code></td>
            <td>Line-height of body text.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>--typeset-flow</code></td>
            <td><code>1.25em</code></td>
            <td>Vertical space between blocks.</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Wiring the fonts</h2>

<p>Webfonts load from the host page and get named as <code>--font-*</code> variables; the
typeset knobs then reference the names, never raw stacks:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;!-- App.razor --&gt;
&lt;link rel="stylesheet" data-blaizio="fonts"
      href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Lora&amp;display=swap"&gt;

/* app.css */
:root {
  --font-lora: "Lora", Georgia, Cambria, serif;
}

.typeset-docs {
  --typeset-font-heading: var(--font-lora);
}</code></pre>

<p>Faces you never override keep flowing from the theme: heading falls back to
<code>--font-heading</code>, mono to <code>--font-mono</code>, body inherits the page.</p>

<h3>Opting out</h3>

<p>Components dropped into the prose mostly defend themselves: their own classes beat the
typeset's zero-specificity rules. For a subtree that must stay untouched entirely, add
<code>not-typeset</code> and the stylesheet skips it and everything inside.</p>

<h3>Wide tables</h3>

<p>Wrap a wide table in <code>typeset-scroll</code> and it scrolls inside its own container
instead of stretching the page. Try it with a reference table and <kbd>Shift</kbd> +
scroll.</p>

<hr />

<p>That is the whole integration: one file, one wrapper, three knobs. The prose you are
reading right now is styled by exactly this stylesheet, tuned by the rail beside it.</p>
